WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.191
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Tuesday, November 10.

2
00:00:09.191 --> 00:00:27.019
Three things today: North Korea tests a new torpedo and sets a 2027 deployment deadline, Seoul's Cabinet approves expanding the Cheonghae anti-piracy unit toward the Strait of Hormuz, and President Lee Jae-myung calls for bipartisan outreach to the new Democratic Congress.

3
00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:35.639
The torpedo test is North Korea's second in a month, and it comes with a named deployment date, which is a different kind of signal.

4
00:00:35.639 --> 00:00:42.430
The National Assembly is expected to hold a plenary session on November 12 to process several key bills.

5
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.867
First, North Korea's second torpedo test in a month.

6
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:59.194
On Monday, North Korea's Academy of Defense Sciences, Missile Administration, and Torpedo Weapon Research Bureau jointly tested what state media called a "new-type high-speed torpedo weapon system."

7
00:00:59.194 --> 00:01:11.085
That was the description in the official KCNA announcement. And the announcement did something the first test report, disclosed after an October 15 test, also did: it named a deployment date.

8
00:01:11.085 --> 00:01:23.598
The Navy will be equipped with the weapon from the beginning of 2027. What's different this time is what's missing. KCNA released no photographs and fewer technical details than the initial disclosure.

9
00:01:23.598 --> 00:01:35.925
That restraint may matter to analysts trying to assess the system's capabilities, but the deployment timeline is the headline. The torpedo test isn't the only North Korean military signal this week.

10
00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:49.621
Kim Yo Jong, the vice department director of the Workers' Party of Korea's Central Committee, issued a sharp public statement rejecting a call by U.N. Secretary-General Antonio Guterres for renewed inter-Korean dialogue.

11
00:01:49.621 --> 00:02:02.881
Guterres had made the appeal following a Demilitarized Zone landmine incident. Kim accused him of taking a "prejudiced and irresponsible attitude" and of "echoing South Korea's unilateral and provocative sophism."

12
00:02:02.881 --> 00:02:15.457
She warned that if what she called "reckless military provocations of South Korea gangsters lead to an undesirable incident in the southern border area," the Secretary-General would bear responsibility.

13
00:02:15.457 --> 00:02:21.060
Reuters also carried the statement. Watch for any South Korean or U.S. response to either.

14
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.379
Second, Seoul moves the Cheonghae unit toward the Strait of Hormuz.

15
00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:40.397
South Korea's Cabinet has approved a motion to expand the Cheonghae anti-piracy unit's deployment zone toward the Hormuz area, and the motion now goes to the National Assembly, where the ruling Democratic Party holds a majority.

16
00:02:40.397 --> 00:02:52.755
The expanded mandate also adds "support for the restoration of freedom of navigation" to the unit's mission. Iran's Foreign Ministry urged Seoul on Monday to refrain from deployment to the Hormuz area.

17
00:02:52.755 --> 00:03:01.301
Seoul reiterated what it called its "non-involvement, non-intervention" principle. The diplomatic groundwork has been building in parallel.

18
00:03:01.301 --> 00:03:11.752
Foreign Minister Cho Hyun, on the sidelines of the second G7 Foreign Ministers' Meeting in Paris, met with Saudi Foreign Minister Faisal bin Farhan Al Saud on November 9.

19
00:03:11.752 --> 00:03:21.835
In a post following that meeting, Cho said both sides agreed that "restoring freedom of navigation based on international law in the Strait of Hormuz is important."

20
00:03:21.835 --> 00:03:27.738
Ministers also agreed to expand cooperation in nuclear energy, advanced technology, and defense.

21
00:03:27.738 --> 00:03:36.222
Cho also held bilaterals with French Foreign Minister Jean-Noël Barrot and Egyptian Foreign Minister Badr Abdelatty at the same gathering.

22
00:03:36.222 --> 00:03:47.473
With France, ministers reviewed the upgrade of bilateral ties to "global strategic partner" status on the 140th anniversary of diplomatic relations. Assembly consent is the next gate.

23
00:03:47.473 --> 00:03:52.330
Watch whether the Democratic majority moves quickly or conditions its approval.

24
00:03:53.530 --> 00:04:05.224
Third, President Lee on the new Democratic Congress. President Lee Jae-myung used a Cabinet meeting to frame the Democratic Party's seizure of both chambers of Congress in last week's U.S.

25
00:04:05.224 --> 00:04:14.990
midterm elections as a moment requiring active Korean management, not passive observation. Lee said South Korea must maintain close cooperation with the U.S.

26
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:20.464
administration while "establishing a bipartisan foundation for cooperation in Congress."

27
00:04:20.464 --> 00:04:32.967
He instructed officials to prepare scenario-based responses to external uncertainties and to minimize negative effects on the domestic economy. Lee's instructions come with some concrete encouragement.

28
00:04:32.967 --> 00:04:43.044
Pro-Korea Democratic lawmakers are emerging as frontrunners to chair key House and Senate foreign affairs and security committees, according to Paijinancial News.

29
00:04:43.044 --> 00:04:51.193
CSIS Korea Chair Victor Cha and colleagues addressed the same dynamics in a press briefing on President Trump's upcoming Asia trip.

30
00:04:51.193 --> 00:05:10.165
They flagged the ASEAN stop in Manila as more substantively significant than the APEC meetings, and noted that the Democratic midterm sweep changes the Congress variable for the alliance's remaining two Trump years, particularly on wartime operational control transfer conditions and security cooperation.

31
00:05:10.165 --> 00:05:28.391
The Diplomat published an analysis arguing that durable U.S.-Korea policy requires bipartisan alignment that transcends which party controls the White House or Congress, and that Democrats gaining leverage over joint exercises and summit concessions is an opportunity to anchor that framework.

32
00:05:28.391 --> 00:05:37.970
The key fact to hold: Lee wants a bipartisan congressional strategy, and the committee chairs who will shape that relationship are still being determined.

33
00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:40.230
Now to Pyongyang.

34
00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:55.009
State media's main domestic story today has Kim Jong Un stressing the importance of agriculture and vowing to improve living conditions in rural areas, continuing a welfare and domestic-messaging theme that has led KCNA for several days.

35
00:05:55.009 --> 00:06:04.425
The torpedo test was confirmed in state media, but the weapons content is absent from internal North Korea news. Kim Yo Jong's statement attacking U.N.

36
00:06:04.425 --> 00:06:13.279
Secretary-General Guterres over the DMZ landmine dispute was the other major output. Kim Jong Un has not made a public appearance in two days.

37
00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:21.760
The bottom line from state media: the torpedo program is being announced outward, while the domestic audience keeps hearing about farms.

38
00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:34.274
The rest of the news, quickly. On personnel, President Lee nominated Lee Yong-cheol, currently director of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as justice minister nominee.

39
00:06:34.274 --> 00:06:44.351
His confirmation hearing preparation team begins work November 11. Media scrutiny has focused on his anticipated stance on prosecution withdrawal of pending cases.

40
00:06:44.351 --> 00:06:52.450
Defense Minister Kang Shin-chul reiterated his push to consolidate South Korea's three service academies into a single institution.

41
00:06:52.450 --> 00:07:03.578
Kang met with cadets from the Army, Navy, and Air Force academies at the Korea Military Academy and said the proposed National Military Academy's location remains under discussion.

42
00:07:03.578 --> 00:07:11.306
South Korea's Ministry of Unification confirmed it has custody of a drifting North Korean fishing vessel taken on November 3.

43
00:07:11.306 --> 00:07:17.550
The ministry has not disclosed the crew size or whether anyone aboard has expressed intent to defect.

44
00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:25.958
The United States has blacklisted a Seychelles-based cryptocurrency exchange over North Korean money laundering, according to N.K. News.

45
00:07:25.958 --> 00:07:41.538
A Pacific Forum analysis identifies three unclosed gaps in South Korea's wartime operational control certification: command authority, network rebuilding, which the analysis says cannot be completed before 2029, and an undefined cyber chain of command.

46
00:07:41.538 --> 00:07:45.680
The annual Security Consultative Meeting has slipped to early 2027.

47
00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:58.106
North Korea moved top artificial intelligence specialists from its spy agency, the Reconnaissance Information General Bureau, to the Foreign Ministry beginning in late October, according to Daily N.K..

48
00:07:58.106 --> 00:08:07.875
The transfers have stirred resentment inside the intelligence unit. Former deputy secretary of state Kurt Campbell said North Korea has communicated that U.S.

49
00:08:07.875 --> 00:08:17.890
recognition of its nuclear status is a precondition for a summit, complicating Trump's Asia-trip diplomacy. South Korea's Financial Security Institute was hacked.

50
00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:26.545
The breach was detected November 9, and the institute notified the Financial Services Commission. Financial firms' security data was leaked.

51
00:08:26.545 --> 00:08:38.230
The International Atomic Energy Agency's Director General Grossi met with South Korean Vice Foreign Minister Jeong Yeon-doo and welcomed Seoul's safeguards commitment on nuclear submarines.

52
00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:48.740
A Gallup Korea poll taken October 27 to 29 put President Lee's approval at 39 percent. The Democratic Party stood at 35 percent, and the People Power Party at 30 percent.

53
00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:50.912
On Capitol Hill.

54
00:08:50.912 --> 00:09:07.311
Analysis from Businesskorea finds that Democratic majorities are likely to pursue stricter data-privacy, climate, and supply-chain regulations that could raise compliance costs for South Korean exporters, even as Democrats are seen as more receptive on alliance matters.

55
00:09:07.311 --> 00:09:19.520
Separately, Representative Young Kim, a Korean-American Republican who co-chaired the Congressional Caucus on Korea, lost her reelection bid, removing a prominent bipartisan Korea voice from the House.

56
00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:26.432
Beyond the region. South Korea plans to announce a 100 million dollar Ukraine aid package.

57
00:09:26.432 --> 00:09:36.904
That commitment aligns with what Foreign Minister Cho Hyun discussed with French Foreign Minister Barrot in Paris, where ministers also reviewed Seoul's planned aid.

58
00:09:36.904 --> 00:09:46.741
Poland received a new delivery of Korean-made K2 main battle tanks and K9 self-propelled howitzers, continuing deliveries under a 2022 framework arms deal.

59
00:09:46.741 --> 00:09:52.770
Poland is among the largest customers of South Korean defense hardware on NATO's eastern flank.

60
00:09:53.970 --> 00:10:08.684
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, surged about 4.4 percent to close at about 5,239, led by chipmakers Samsung and SK Hynix on dip buying after the previous session's selloff.

61
00:10:08.684 --> 00:10:21.328
The won strengthened, with the dollar buying about 1,339 won. Brent crude fell about 2 percent to around 90 dollars. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Tuesday, November 10.

62
00:10:21.328 --> 00:10:26.060
The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
